+# PlacementRules spec cleanup
+
+`PlacementRules.spec.luau` now tests only what `PlacementRules.luau` documents, and only through
+its public API (`snap`, `validate`, `MaxHeight`, `MaxReach`). `PlacementRules.luau` is unchanged.
+It went from 31 tests to 33, counting each `it.each` row as one test. 7 weak tests were removed
+and 9 were added, mostly for rules that had no coverage.
+
+## The two failing tests
+
+- **"rejects a position that isn't a Vector3"** called the matchers with a colon:
+  `expect(ok):toBe(false)`. Jest Lua matchers take a dot. With a colon, the expectation object
+  gets passed as the expected value, so the test failed even though `validate` behaves correctly.
+  Switched to `.toBe`.
+- **"doesn't mark the cell occupied"** counted the entries in the table and asserted
+  `expect(count).toBeFalsy()`. In Lua, `0` is truthy (only `false` and `nil` are falsy), so the
+  test failed exactly when the code was right. The test now checks that the caller's table
+  is unchanged after an accepted placement.
+
+## Removed
+
+| Test | Why |
+|---|---|
+| `snap` "snaps positions to cell centers" | It rebuilt the result with the same formula the module uses, so it can't catch a wrong formula. Its sample `(5, 9, -3)` was moved into the `snap` table with a hand-worked answer. |
+| `_isFinite` × 3 | They tested a private helper that is only exposed for the spec. Renaming or inlining it broke the suite while placement still worked. NaN and ±infinity are now tested through `validate`, which is the documented rule. (`_isFinite` is still exported because I wasn't allowed to change the module. It can go once nothing uses it.) |
+| "calls snap exactly once" | It spied on how `validate` is built, not on what it does. Caching or inlining the snap broke it. "Returns the cell's center" is tested by value instead. |
+| "rejects placing twice in the same cell" | It only passed because an earlier test had written to a shared `occupied` table, so it depended on test order. Merged into the occupied-cell test with its own table. |
+| `BlockTypes` "has the right block types" | It checked every color and material in a different module, so any art tweak broke the PlacementRules suite. The only BlockTypes behavior placement depends on is "known names are accepted", and that's still covered by looping over `BlockTypes.List`. |
+
+## Changed
+
+- **No shared state.** The file-level `occupied` table is gone. Every test makes its own table, so
+  tests can run in any order or one at a time.
+- **"returns the snapped center"** now compares against a fixed answer, `(6, 2, -2)`, instead of
+  `snap(position)`.
+- **Height** rejections now also check the reason (`"out of height range"`). The root is placed
+  level with the cell so that reach can't be what rejects it.
+- **`jest` import** was removed because nothing uses it now.
+
+## Added (rules that had no coverage)
+
+- `rootPosition == nil` is rejected with `"no character"`.
+- `+inf` and `-inf` components are rejected (before, only NaN was tested).
+- Block type names that aren't strings (`nil`, number, table) are rejected. The module says it's
+  safe to call with raw remote arguments.
+- A free cell next to an occupied one is accepted.
+- One guard test checks that `BlockSize`, `MaxHeight`, and `MaxReach` are 4, 200, and 24.
+
+## Rule coverage
+
+| Documented rule | Tests |
+|---|---|
+| `snap` returns the center of the containing cell, aligned to the origin, with the Baseplate cell at Y = BlockSize/2 | `snap` table |
+| blockTypeName names a BlockTypes type | block type accepts / rejects |
+| position is a finite Vector3 | position: not a Vector3, NaN, ±inf |
+| cell center above the Baseplate and ≤ MaxHeight | height table |
+| player has a character | "rejects a player with no character" |
+| within MaxReach, with exactly MaxReach counting as in reach | reach table (20 / 24 / 28 studs) |
+| cell not already occupied | occupied cells |
+| returns true and the center, or false and a reason | return-value and reason checks throughout |
+| never changes `occupied` | "doesn't change occupied when accepting" |
+
+## Assumptions
+
+- **Numbers are hard-coded.** The boundary cases use the current values (block size 4, max
+  height 200, reach 24) because hand-worked numbers are easier to check than numbers computed from
+  the constants. The guard test fails first, with a clear message, if one of those values is
+  tuned.
+- **Reason strings are asserted.** One request can break more than one rule, and checking the
+  reason is the only way to know which rule rejected it. The cost is that rewording a log message
+  means updating the spec too. I think that's worth it.
+- **The doc and the code disagree slightly on height.** The doc says the cell's center must be
+  `Y > 0`, but the code checks `center.Y < 0`. Cell centers are always odd multiples of
+  `BlockSize / 2`, so a center can never be exactly 0 and the two behave the same. I didn't add a
+  test for it.
+- **The specs haven't run.** They can't run here. luau-lsp, selene, and StyLua all pass.
